  envelope for a single string is complexity bought against a migration that may never come. If the format ever does
  need to change, the [3mkey name[0m is the version lever: write [38;5;153mapp.userId.v2[39m, read both during a transition, drop the old
  key. Same headroom, no ceremony now.

  [1mStorage can fail.[0m [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m throws in private-browsing modes, when disabled by policy, and on quota. Every access
  is wrapped; on failure the module keeps the value in a module-level variable for the page's lifetime. The app degrades
  to "works this visit, forgets on reload" rather than breaking the login form. This is the main reason storage access
  is centralized at all.

  [1mNot an auth credential.[0m Written into the module's header comment and the spec: this value is a correlation key. Any
  code on the origin can read or forge it. If it ever needs to authenticate, it moves to an [38;5;153mhttpOnly[39m cookie and the
  client stops reading it.

  [1mSection 2 — The login contract[0m

  Pinned in the spec for a real backend to honor later:

  POST  https://api.example.com/login
  body  { "username": string, "password": string }

  200   { "success": true,  "user": string, "userId": string }
        userId: opaque, non-empty, server-assigned, stable per account.
                    Clients must not parse or derive meaning from it.
  4xx   { "success": false, "error": string }   // no userId

  [1mOne consequence worth your explicit sign-off:[0m a real [38;5;153mfetch[39m is asynchronous, so I'd make [38;5;153mlogin()[39m [38;5;153masync[39m [1mnow[0m, while the
  stub still returns a canned object. The submit handler at [38;5;153mapp.js:23[39m becomes [38;5;153masync[39m and [38;5;153mawait[39ms it. If we leave [38;5;153mlogin()[39m
  synchronous, then dropping in the real network call later changes the signature [3mand[0m every caller — a breaking change
  deferred rather than avoided. Doing it now costs two keywords and makes the eventual swap a body-only edit.

  The stub returns a fixed placeholder [38;5;153muserId[39m so the storage path is exercised end to end from day one.

  Does this hold up? Say the word and I'll lay out module loading, error handling, and the test plan.

